<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arthur Evans</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1851">1851</a>–<a href="/years/1941">1941</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sir Arthur Evans</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Arthur Evans was a British archaeologist who excavated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> from 1900 and partly reconstructed it in concrete. He named the civilisation he uncovered 'Minoan' after the legendary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minos">King Minos</a>, devised its chronology and identified the scripts <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-a">Linear A</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a>. He was keeper of the Ashmolean Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arthur-evans.jpg" alt="Arthur Evans" width="320" height="437" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bassano Ltd · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arthur_Evans_in_1939.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Arthur Evans was born on 8 July 1851 at Nash Mills, Hemel Hempstead, the first child of John Evans and Harriet Ann Dickinson. His father worked in the paper mill owned by the family of Harriet's father, John Dickinson, and profits from the mill later helped fund Evans's excavations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a> and his publications. He entered Harrow School in 1865 and matriculated at Brasenose College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, on 9 June 1870, where he read Modern History. His sight was blurry beyond a close distance, and he carried a cane he called Prodger to explore his surroundings.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Evans returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> with his wife Margaret in January 1883 and became Keeper of the Ashmolean Museum, then in a chaotic state of transition. He led the excavation of an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> cemetery at Aylesford in Kent, published in 1890, and concluded that its culture was closely related to the continental Belgae, which remains the modern view. In 1898 he reported for the Manchester Guardian on the violence against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>'s Muslim minority and condemned religiously motivated violence from both sides.</p><p>The first excavation at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a> was begun in 1877 by Minos Kalokairinos, until the Ottoman authorities stopped it. Evans bought the land with private funds and began work in March 1900, with Duncan Mackenzie and the architect Fyfe and 32 diggers. Within months they uncovered a large part of what he called the Palace of Minos, an intricate set of over 1,000 interlocking rooms that included workshops and wine presses and served as a storage, religious and administrative centre. He resigned as Keeper in 1908, became president of the Society of Antiquaries in 1914, and was knighted in 1911.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>From what he found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a> and across the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, Evans concluded that he must distinguish the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minoan-civilisation">Minoan civilisation</a> from Mycenaean Greece. He found about 3,000 clay tablets, which he transcribed and published in Scripta Minoa; some are now missing, so his transcriptions are the only record of their marks. He saw that the scripts were two separate systems, which he named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-a">Linear A</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a>, with A the earlier, and he also defined an earlier pictographic writing. Linear B proved to be Greek, and Linear A has not been convincingly deciphered. His classifications and transcriptions have been of great value to scholars of Mycenaean Greece.</p><p>He was elected a Fellow of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> and a founding Fellow of the British Academy in 1901. He lived at Youlbury from 1894 until his death in 1941 and left part of his estate to the Boy Scouts.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arthur Evans” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its greatest modern impact came from archaeology: excavation of the Minoan palace by <b>Arthur Evans</b> from 1900 revealed the Bronze Age civilisation he named Minoan, while later work by the British School at Athens has made Iron Age Knossos one of the best known Greek towns of its…</blockquote><a href="/empire/knossos">Knossos · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Arthur Evans</b>, who excavated Knossos from 1900, popularised the name “Minoan” after the mythical King Minos and divided Cretan prehistory into Early, Middle and Late Minoan phases on the evidence of pottery styles.</blockquote><a href="/culture/minoan-civilisation">Minoan civilisation · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Arthur Evans</b> excavated and partly reconstructed the site from 1900.</blockquote><a href="/state/palace-state-of-knossos">Palace State of Knossos</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Nash Mills, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Youlbury House, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.73,-0.45&amp;year=1909" aria-label="Nash Mills on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4540 374 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Arthur Evans"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4990 644h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4990 644h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.73,-0.45&amp;year=1909">What was here in 1909? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
